        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mithraism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 80 CE – c. 400 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, where its earliest temples appear in the last quarter of the 1st century CE, apparently with its centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; inspired by Iranian worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>, though how far it continued Persian practice is debated</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none known: Merkelbach, Clauss and Beck think a founder or founders created the mysteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, while others see a continuation of Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a> worship</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">none survive: no written narratives or theology, only an oath, a catechism fragment, graffiti and inscriptions, and passing references in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writers; the histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> by Pallas and Euboulus are lost</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: persecuted by Christians in the 4th century, it was suppressed and came to an end between the last decade of that century and the 5th century</span></div>
        </div>
