    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Mithraism</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut.jpg" alt="Mount Nemrut" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Mount Nemrut <span class="kid-tale-cr">Klearchos Kapoutsis from Santorini, Greece, CC BY 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Roman god Mithras took his name from Mithra, an old god of Iran.</p><p>At Mount Nemrut, a king set up giant statues where Mithras sat among the gods.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>More than 1,900 years ago, the worship of Mithras began in the Roman Empire, probably centred in Rome.</p><p>Scholars still argue about how much of it came from Persia.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithra.jpg" alt="Mithra" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Mithra <span class="kid-tale-cr">Philippe Chavin, CC BY 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Every temple had a picture of Mithras killing a bull, looking back towards the sun god Sol.</p><p>Followers met for shared meals in small temples underground, made to look like a cave.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Many followers were soldiers, and the religion spread along the Rhine and Danube rivers and to Britain.</p><p>Followers passed through seven grades, or steps, and joined by shaking hands with their leader.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>More than 1,600 years ago, Christians persecuted its followers, and the religion came to an end.</p><p>What remains are its temples, carvings, writing on temple walls and a few scraps of papyrus.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Initiates, the syndexioi or “those united by the handshake”, passed through seven grades and met for ritual meals in small underground temples, the mithraea, made to represent the cave where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> killed the bull.</li><li>Soldiers were strongly represented, with merchants, customs officials, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> and slaves, and the cult spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Ostia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontiers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</li><li>Its origins are still debated: Franz Cumont saw it as the Roman form of the Persian religion, but since the 1970s scholars have generally rejected his model, many seeing a new creation of the Roman world.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Votive coins scattered over the floor of the mithraeum at Pons Sarravi (Sarrebourg) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Gallia Belgica</a> run from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>: when the temple was destroyed, Christians apparently regarded the coins as polluted and left them behind.</p></aside>
    </section>

