    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">67 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Cilician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> are said to practise “secret rites” of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a>, though whether this bears on the origins of the mysteries is unclear</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">66</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiridates-i-of-armenia">Tiridates I of Armenia</a>, crowned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, tells the emperor that he reveres him “as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a>”</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 80</span><span class="rf-text">Statius’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a>, one of the earliest literary references, pictures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> in a cave; the first mithraea appear</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 145</span><span class="rf-text">Justin Martyr charges the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> with imitating the Christian communion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">184</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virunum">Virunum</a> album records a Mithraic festival of commemoration on 26 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">4th century</span><span class="rf-text">Mithraists face persecution from Christians, and their sanctuaries are destroyed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 395</span><span class="rf-text">Votive coins at Sarrebourg end with the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, near the end of the cult</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1971</span><span class="rf-text">At the First International Congress of Mithraic Studies, John R. Hinnells and R. L. Gordon severely criticise Cumont’s theory of Persian origins</span></li></ol>
    </section>

