    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut.jpg" alt="Mount Nemrut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut">Mount Nemrut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Nemrut_-_East_Terrace_(4961323529).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Klearchos Kapoutsis from Santorini, Greece, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> takes his name from Mithra, an old pre-Zoroastrian and later Zoroastrian god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; the related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a> is praised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, and the form mi-it-ra- appears in a treaty between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> of about 1400 BCE. In the 1st century BCE King Antiochus I of Commagene set up colossal statues at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut">Mount Nemrut</a> in which Mithras, beardless and in Parthian dress, sat among the gods, named Apollo-Mithras-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>, but this shows reverence for Mithras, not the mysteries. Whether these names refer to one god is disputed: John R. Hinnells writes of a single deity, while David Ulansey sees the bull-slaying Mithras as a new god given an old name.</p><p>The origins of the mysteries are intensely debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> wrote that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> celebrated secret rites of Mithras, and Ulansey dates the first evidence to the middle of the 1st century BCE; Manfred Clauss holds that the mysteries were not practised until the 1st century CE, and C. M. Daniels finds the link unclear. Their underground temples appear suddenly in the archaeology in the last quarter of the 1st century CE.</p><p>Merkelbach suggested that a Greek-speaking founder from an eastern province created the mysteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, perhaps among the imperial bureaucracy; Beck calls this the most likely scenario, and Lewis M. Hopfe finds the cult’s epicentre in Rome. A. D. H. Bivar, L. A. Campbell and G. Widengren instead see a continuation of Iranian Mithra worship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mithra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithra.jpg" alt="Mithra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taq-e_Bostan_-_High-relief_of_Ardeshir_II_investiture.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe Chavin, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the heart of every temple was the image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> killing a bull, the tauroctony. In Anatolian dress and a Phrygian cap he kneels on the exhausted bull, holds it by the nostrils and stabs it, looking back towards the sun god Sol; a dog and a snake reach for the blood, a scorpion seizes the bull’s genitals, ears of wheat spring from its tail, and the torch-bearers Cautes and Cautopates stand on either side. Other scenes show Mithras born from a rock, striking water from stone, shaking hands with Sol, feasting with him on the bull’s hide and rising to heaven in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>. A lion-headed figure entwined by a serpent, perhaps named Arimanius, is generally thought to be linked with time and the seasons.</p><p>Initiates swore an oath of secrecy. Most rituals involved feasting, and many seem to have re-enacted episodes of the Mithras narrative, with the senior officers playing Mithras and Sol. Prayers were addressed to the Sun three times a day, and Sunday was especially sacred. Cherry stones point to midsummer festivities; whether Mithras’s birthday fell on 25 December is disputed.</p><p>The temples, mithraea, were small, windowless and underground, representing the cave of the bull-slaying, often near springs, with benches for 15 to 30 diners. Porphyry reports moral demands on initiates of the Lion grade, and Julian speaks of “commandments of Mithras”, but doctrine may have varied from place to place, with no central authority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No written narratives or theology of the religion survive. Apart from an oath and a fragment of a catechism for the Lion grade on an Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>, the main Mithraic texts are the graffiti on temple walls and the inscriptions on monuments, about 1,000 of them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> Liturgy, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Greek Magical Papyrus from 4th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, calls Mithras “the great god” and identifies him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>, but scholars disagree whether it is Mithraic: Franz Cumont thought not, Marvin Meyer thinks so, and Hans Dieter Betz sees a blend of Greek, Egyptian and Mithraic traditions. Monographs on Mithras by Pallas and Euboulus are lost.</p><p>Outside writers give only glimpses. According to Mary Boyce the earliest literary references are by Statius, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a> (c. 80 CE) pictures Mithras in a cave wrestling with something horned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> (c. 100 CE). Justin Martyr, about 145, charged the cult with imitating the Christian communion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> lists the seven grades, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a> and Firmicus Maternus attacked the cult.</p><p>In his Cave of the Nymphs the philosopher Porphyry, citing Euboulus, wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a> consecrated a natural cave in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to Mithras, creator of the world, and linked Mithras with Aries and Mars. Robert Turcan doubts that Porphyry reports what Mithraists believed, while Roger Beck holds his work the only clear ancient text on the intent of the mysteries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The earliest traces lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and on the frontiers. An altar on the Esquiline in Rome to Sol Invictus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> by the imperial freedman T. Flavius Hyginus probably dates from 80–100 CE, and the earliest bull-slaying monument, also from Rome, from 98–99 at the earliest. Roger Beck lists the places of the earliest phase (c. 80–120 CE): Rome, Nida and Mogontiacum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, Pons Aeni in Noricum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novae">Novae</a> and Oescus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, and Caesarea Maritima in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>. The earliest military inscription, by a centurion of the Fifteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, comes from Carnuntum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, probably before 114; C. M. Daniels names the Danube and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as the regions where the cult first took root. Altars of 140 CE at Inveresk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> are believed to come from the empire’s most northerly temple of Mithras.</p><p>The first important expansion came late in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, and the cult reached the height of its popularity in the 2nd and 3rd centuries, spreading at an “astonishing” rate. Temples were numerous in Rome, Ostia, Numidia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Danube, as far east as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, and fewer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, where Hopfe counts only three; Dura-Europos marks the cult’s eastern limit. About 420 sites have yielded remains. F. Coarelli estimated that Rome had not less than 680–690 mithraea, while Leonard Boyle counted fifty known in the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Initiates called themselves syndexioi, “those united by the handshake”: admission ended with a handshake with the pater, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> had shaken hands with Sol. There were seven grades of initiation, which Manfred Clauss connects with the planets; a mosaic in the Mithraeum of Felicissimus at Ostia shows them with their emblems. The highest, pater, is the commonest on inscriptions. Each grade seems to have demanded an ordeal of heat, cold or peril: an ordeal pit of the early 3rd century was found at Carrawburgh, though by the later 3rd century such pits were floored over. Clauss, noting that only about 14% of names inscribed before 250 CE give a grade, argues that the grades were a class of priests; Richard Gordon holds the older view that every initiate had one.</p><p>Membership lists, such as the graffiti of Dura-Europos and the bronze album of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virunum">Virunum</a>, name only men, and Gordon concludes that the cult was for men only, though Porphyry refers to female initiates and David Jonathan has suggested that women took part in some places. Soldiers were strongly represented, with merchants, customs officials, minor bureaucrats and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> and slaves; few came from aristocratic or senatorial families before the pagan revival of the mid-4th century. Each temple had its own officers, and initiates could move with their grades from one mithraeum to another. The small congregations may have served, like the Roman collegia, as clubs for the unclubbed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mithraism is viewed as a rival of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Christian apologists noted similarities and called them devilish imitations: Justin Martyr compared its bread and cup with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a> likened its ritual bath and mark on the forehead to baptism. In the 4th century Mithraists faced persecution from Christians, and the religion ended between that century’s last decade and the 5th century. L. H. Martin dates its end to the anti-pagan decrees of Theodosius; Beck holds that early in the century it was already as good as dead, though senators of the pagan revival still named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> among their cults. Coins at Sarrebourg show its temple in use until near the century’s end, and Cumont thought the cult may have survived in remote cantons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> into the 5th century. Churches such as Santa Prisca and San Clemente were built over mithraea, and Mithras, unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, was forgotten in late antiquity.</p><p>Modern study began with Franz Cumont’s collection of texts and monuments (1894–1900), which presented the cult as the Roman form of Mazdaism, the Persian state religion. At the First International Congress of Mithraic Studies in 1971 John R. Hinnells and R. L. Gordon severely criticised this view. In 1882 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a> wrote that had Christianity been arrested, “the world would have been Mithraic”; Leonard Boyle and J. A. Ezquerra think the threat overstated.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">In the Research Series</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move<span>Essay</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mithraism was a Roman mystery religion of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a>, whose initiates, many of them soldiers, passed through seven grades and feasted in small cave-like temples centred on the image of Mithras slaying a bull. It spread through the western empire from the late 1st century CE under an Iranian god’s name, though how much else came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> is debated. Christians suppressed it by the end of the 4th century.</em></p>

